From a9f05e8eb8d84cefcc51fbadc8c341b6ce1a4f2e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Martin Lonkwitz <mawelo@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 13:30:58 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] [AI generated] Scripting API (Lua 5.4) and MCP server
 over stdio (btop --mcp-stdio)

Layers: core API -> Lua binding -> adapter (MCP). The adapter holds no logic
of its own; everything it offers is reachable from Lua.

* src/btop_core.{hpp,cpp}: UI-free core. Core::Loop collects in its own
  thread and publishes immutable snapshots (CPU, memory, disks, network,
  processes); Core::send_signal is the one writing command. The menu sends
  signals through it as well.
* src/btop_lua.{hpp,cpp}: module "btop" (version, snapshot_info, cpu, memory,
  disks, network, processes, sort_keys, send_signal) in a sandboxed state:
  no io/os/package/debug, no dofile/loadfile, memory limit (64 MiB) by the
  allocator, time limit (2 s per call) by an instruction hook. Reference:
  docs/lua-api.md.
* libs/lua-5.4.8: Lua 5.4.8, unchanged upstream sources (MIT), compiled as
  C++ so that a Lua error unwinds C++ frames instead of longjmp over them.
  ORIGIN.txt holds the download URL and the sha256 sum.
* src/btop_mcp.{hpp,cpp}, src/btop_mcp_tools.hpp: btop --mcp-stdio runs
  without a terminal and answers MCP clients (JSON-RPC 2.0, newline-delimited,
  on stdin/stdout). The tools are the Lua script mcp/tools.lua (installed as
  share/btop/mcp/tools.lua; other file: --mcp-tools <file>); SIGHUP reloads it.
  send_signal is offered only with --mcp-allow-signals. Reader, Lua worker
  and collector run in separate threads, so a tool call never blocks the
  reader. Tool list: docs/mcp.md (generated by tools/doc/gen_mcp_md.lua).
* libs/reuse: shared single headers. mcp_stdio_server.hpp (MCP skeleton for
  stdio), mcp_dispatcher.hpp (JSON-RPC methods of MCP), lua_sandbox.hpp (the
  sandboxed Lua state with both limits), lua_mcp_tools.hpp (tool registry
  from a Lua script), lua_json_bridge.hpp (JSON <-> Lua values) and
  nlohmann/json 3.x (json.hpp, MIT).
* src/btop_cli.{hpp,cpp}: options --mcp-stdio, --mcp-tools, --mcp-allow-signals.
* docs/btop_core.md, docs/btop_lua.md, docs/btop_mcp.md: purpose, flow,
  correctness and security of each layer.
* tests: GTest suites core_loop, lua_api and mcp_roundtrip (initialize,
  tools/list, tools/call, errors, reload against the real mcp/tools.lua).

Security: stdio only, so the process boundary is the trust boundary; whoever
may start btop --mcp-stdio (for example over ssh) sees what the btop user
sees. No network port is opened.
